      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Tarrowby Logistics, a fictional haulier in West Yorkshire. It's your break at the Shipley depot, and six things are waiting on your work phone: texts, two printed notices you passed on site, and an email. Some are real and some were built to trick you. Sort them, and report or check anything that doesn't sit right. Reporting a real message never costs you anything.",
        "flipMissedHeading": "Nothing to replay this time",
        "flipLandedNoTells": "It landed clean on the phone, with nothing in it to flag. Next, the same kind of message turns up on a Tarrowby work phone, and you're the one reading it.",
        "noBlameLine": "Being caught by one of these is no fault of yours. These were built to look like Tarrowby's own messages, and they land on a phone you're reading between jobs. Catching them is a habit, not a test of how clever you are.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: if a text, a notice or an email wants you to scan a code or tap a short link to pay, sign in or confirm something, stop. Don't reply, not even STOP. Open the app or site the way you normally would, or ring the sender on a number you already hold, then report the message the way your organisation asks you to.",
        "defendOnly": "These landed on a phone between jobs, and they were built to read like Tarrowby's own messages, so anyone can be caught by one. Any you were caught out by are shown below, with what gave each one away.",
        "defendIntro": "Go through the work phone and decide what to do with each text, notice and email. On a notice, check where the code goes before you would scan it. There's no clock on you here; the whole job is one or two small habits."
      },
